    "updated_at": "2026-09-21T12:37:06Z",
    "body": "> That seems very strange and I doubt that matches implementations (for the script case).\n\nI had a look at this with this example:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n\n<math>\n  <annotation-xml id=\"annotation\" encoding=\"text/html\">\n    <unknown id=\"element1\"></unknown>\n    <script>annotation.removeAttribute(\"encoding\")</script>\n    <unknown id=\"element2\"></unknown>\n  </annotation-xml>\n</math>\n\n<script>\n  console.log(element1.namespaceURI);\n  console.log(element2.namespaceURI);    \n</script>\n```\n\nIt looks like all browsers (tested on Chrome, Firefox and Epiphany (WebKit)) follow the spec and print the HTML namespace in both cases.\n\n> But does that mean that if the sanitizer sanitized the attribute (or script removed it) it doesn't matter because the start tag it had is still the same?\n\nThat is my understanding of the spec, and seems sensible to me, especially in conjunction with things [streaming HTML](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12758/changes). (I don't think changing the parsing mode at a potentially arbitrary point in the stream is likely to be anything but a bug in virtually all cases).",
